\section*{Two-step linear images of special quasi-projective groups}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every complex linear representation of a smooth special quasi-projective fundamental group is claimed to have virtually two-step-nilpotent image, while the paper also constructs nonspecial general quasi-Albanese fibers. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For a smooth complex quasi-projective $V$, specialness requires the following after any proper birational modification: each dominant algebraic fibration onto a normal quasi-projective base $Z$ with $\dim Z>0$ and connected general fiber has $\kappa(Z,\Delta_f)<\dim Z$, where $\Delta_f$ denotes the induced orbifold boundary. The orbifold dimension is computed on smooth compactifications with infimum multiplicities, and removed divisors have infinite multiplicity. For every connected such $V$, every $d\ge1$ and every $\rho:\pi_1(V)\to\operatorname{GL}_d(\mathbb C)$, OpenAI claims
\[
\exists H\le\rho(\pi_1V):\quad[\rho(\pi_1V):H]<\infty,\qquad[H,[H,H]]=1.
\]
Thus every complex linear image is virtually nilpotent of class at most two. Virtual abelianity of the entire open fundamental group is not the stated conclusion.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 057, 2026-09-24): At each canonical nilpotent stage, the fiber Lie algebra equals the commutator algebra. Its abelianization has only weight minus two, making the next conjugation bracket incompatible with the allowed weights; universality then transfers the resulting class-two bound to arbitrary unipotent representations.

